1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,535
CI, the automatic testing of every change,
was green on the pull request, your

2
00:00:09,575 --> 00:00:14,675
proposal to merge a branch into main. Ten
minutes after the merge it was red on

3
00:00:14,715 --> 00:00:18,840
main. Nobody pushed anything in
between except the merge itself.

4
00:00:18,880 --> 00:00:20,922
Your CTO asks: what did CI test?

5
00:00:21,262 --> 00:00:26,228
It tested a commit, one saved snapshot of
the project, that is on no branch and in

6
00:00:26,268 --> 00:00:31,296
nobody's clone: a test merge that GitHub
made, of your branch with main as main was

7
00:00:31,336 --> 00:00:35,875
at that moment. main moved on before
you merged, and the test merge was not

8
00:00:35,915 --> 00:00:36,608
regenerated.

9
00:00:36,948 --> 00:00:41,579
To answer questions like this one you need
to know exactly what a pull request is

10
00:00:41,619 --> 00:00:45,789
made of, and which three commits every
part of its page is computed from.

11
00:00:45,829 --> 00:00:49,710
Keep that green check in mind. In
the demo you'll watch it go stale.

12
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:55,428
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:55,468 --> 00:00:59,410
This is the first of five videos
on pull requests, Chapter 17.

14
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:04,548
Hold on to one sentence through all of
them, from the first page of the chapter:

15
00:01:04,888 --> 00:01:08,691
The page changes when one of the three
changes, and for no other reason.

16
00:01:09,031 --> 00:01:13,162
Today, two questions. What does GitHub
create when a pull request opens?

17
00:01:13,202 --> 00:01:16,812
And what do the "Commits" tab and
the "Files changed" tab show?

18
00:01:17,152 --> 00:01:22,318
The sample project is ticket-router, a small
service that reads a support ticket and

19
00:01:22,358 --> 00:01:27,090
names the queue that should answer it.
A bare repository on disk, one with no

20
00:01:27,130 --> 00:01:31,243
working tree, plays the shared
repository. You are the contributor.

21
00:01:31,283 --> 00:01:35,023
Asha maintains the repository
and merges. Ravi is a teammate.

22
00:01:35,363 --> 00:01:40,561
Two replays, labs/ch17/pr-refs.sh
and labs/ch17/pr-anatomy.sh.

23
00:01:40,601 --> 00:01:47,320
One caution about the first: the commands
on the "server" are plain Git that the

24
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:52,135
textbook's author chose to
produce the documented result.

25
00:01:52,175 --> 00:01:55,937
GitHub does not publish
the commands it runs.

26
00:01:56,277 --> 00:02:01,734
Labels: opening a pull request, gh pr
create, is 🟡 CAUTION: it creates a GitHub

27
00:02:01,774 --> 00:02:05,910
object and starts review requests,
checks and notifications.

28
00:02:05,950 --> 00:02:08,972
git fetch origin
pull/N/head:pr-N is 🟢 SAFE.

29
00:02:10,812 --> 00:02:12,224
After this video you can:

30
00:02:12,564 --> 00:02:17,326
List what GitHub creates when a pull request
is opened and which parts are Git data.

31
00:02:17,666 --> 00:02:19,970
Fetch a pull request's
head with plain Git.

32
00:02:20,310 --> 00:02:24,504
Explain the test merge commit, when it is
regenerated and what it means when it is

33
00:02:24,544 --> 00:02:24,865
absent.

34
00:02:25,205 --> 00:02:29,984
Explain why the commit list is a two-dot
range and the diff a three-dot comparison.

35
00:02:30,324 --> 00:02:34,731
Construct a case where the two differ and
say how merging the base into the branch

36
00:02:34,771 --> 00:02:35,436
changes each.

37
00:02:37,276 --> 00:02:40,584
In one sentence: opening a
pull request moves no branch.

38
00:02:40,624 --> 00:02:45,607
It creates a GitHub object and a little Git
data in the base repository: a read-only

39
00:02:45,647 --> 00:02:50,031
ref for the head commit and, when the
merge is clean, a test merge commit.

40
00:02:50,071 --> 00:02:54,695
The base is the branch you merge into,
here main. The head is the branch whose

41
00:02:54,735 --> 00:02:58,163
commits you offer. A ref is a
name that holds a commit ID.

42
00:02:58,503 --> 00:03:01,467
You should be able to say
which layer owns each item.

43
00:03:01,807 --> 00:03:04,789
Your commits, the head
branch, the base branch: Git.

44
00:03:04,829 --> 00:03:07,753
Objects and refs in the
head and base repositories.

45
00:03:08,093 --> 00:03:13,740
The pull request number, title, description,
labels, reviewers, reviews, comments,

46
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:17,347
review threads: GitHub's
database, not in any clone.

47
00:03:17,687 --> 00:03:20,737
refs/pull/<number>/head:
Git data written by GitHub.

48
00:03:20,777 --> 00:03:23,588
A ref in the base repository,
read-only for you.

49
00:03:23,928 --> 00:03:28,139
The test merge commit and refs/pull/<number>/merge:
Git data written by GitHub.

50
00:03:28,179 --> 00:03:31,314
A commit and a ref in the base
repository, "when possible".

51
00:03:31,654 --> 00:03:36,445
Checks and commit statuses: GitHub and
Actions, attached to commit IDs in GitHub's

52
00:03:36,485 --> 00:03:36,976
database.

53
00:03:37,316 --> 00:03:41,548
The "Commits" and "Files changed" tabs:
GitHub, computed from the three commits,

54
00:03:41,588 --> 00:03:42,723
stored nowhere in Git.

55
00:03:43,063 --> 00:03:47,999
GitHub's reference says: "When you open a
pull request, GitHub creates temporary Git

56
00:03:48,039 --> 00:03:52,738
references that point to the pull request's
head branch and, when possible, to a

57
00:03:52,778 --> 00:03:56,826
simulated merge result." The page
no longer spells out the ref names.

58
00:03:56,866 --> 00:04:01,802
Two other pages of the documentation confirm
them: the checkout instructions and the

59
00:04:01,842 --> 00:04:03,106
Actions documentation.

60
00:04:03,446 --> 00:04:08,246
The test merge. GitHub "creates a merge
commit to test whether the pull request can

61
00:04:08,286 --> 00:04:13,028
be automatically merged into the base
branch. This test commit is not added to the

62
00:04:13,068 --> 00:04:17,577
base branch or the head branch". Its
parents are the latest commit on the base

63
00:04:17,617 --> 00:04:21,834
branch and the pull request's head
commit. In our picture that's 135aad1.

64
00:04:22,174 --> 00:04:27,140
Three facts about it. First, CI uses it.
For a pull_request event, the Actions

65
00:04:27,180 --> 00:04:31,953
documentation states that the checkout
action "checks out the merge branch.

66
00:04:31,993 --> 00:04:36,894
Your CI tests run against the merged
result, not just the head branch alone".

67
00:04:37,234 --> 00:04:42,620
Second, it is a stored commit, so it can
be stale: main moves on, and its parent is

68
00:04:42,660 --> 00:04:47,849
still the old tip. The version note: before
the nineteenth of February 2026, the

69
00:04:47,889 --> 00:04:53,209
test merge commit was also regenerated when
somebody viewed the pull request page.

70
00:04:53,549 --> 00:04:58,433
Since then, it is generated only when changes
are pushed to the pull request branch,

71
00:04:58,473 --> 00:05:03,299
when the merge base changes, or when the
current test merge commit is older than 12

72
00:05:03,339 --> 00:05:07,109
hours. Do not build automation on
the freshness of the merge ref.

73
00:05:07,449 --> 00:05:11,707
One caveat marked unverified in the book:
a REST guide still says the commit "is

74
00:05:11,747 --> 00:05:16,006
created when you view the pull request in
the UI". That conflicts with the later

75
00:05:16,046 --> 00:05:17,940
changelog, which the course follows.

76
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:21,647
Third, when the merge conflicts,
there is no merge ref.

77
00:05:21,687 --> 00:05:26,727
Two documented consequences: the merge
button is deactivated until the conflict is

78
00:05:26,767 --> 00:05:30,569
resolved, and workflows will not
run on pull_request activity.

79
00:05:30,609 --> 00:05:35,091
A pull request with no CI run at all
is often a conflicting pull request.

80
00:05:35,431 --> 00:05:40,072
Two more properties. The head ref outlives
the head branch: delete the branch, and

81
00:05:40,112 --> 00:05:44,752
the commits are still one fetch away. Nobody
can rewrite or delete these refs with

82
00:05:44,792 --> 00:05:49,090
Git. And the base is pinned: GitHub
"sets the base to the commit that branch

83
00:05:49,130 --> 00:05:53,884
references" when you open the pull request,
and does not update that commit when the

84
00:05:53,924 --> 00:05:58,336
branch moves. That is why the pull request
page and a compare page of the same

85
00:05:58,376 --> 00:06:00,277
branches can show different diffs.

86
00:06:00,617 --> 00:06:04,024
Now the second question, with
both branches on screen.

87
00:06:04,064 --> 00:06:09,068
In one sentence: the "Commits" tab is the
output of git log base..head, and the

88
00:06:09,108 --> 00:06:14,175
"Files changed" tab is the output of git
diff base...head, a diff from the merge

89
00:06:14,215 --> 00:06:19,154
base to the head. The merge base is the
most recent commit that both histories

90
00:06:19,194 --> 00:06:20,623
contain: here, 9a383e5.

91
00:06:20,963 --> 00:06:26,344
You learned both notations in the history
videos. For git log, A..B means "reachable

92
00:06:26,384 --> 00:06:31,443
from B and not from A". For git diff,
which compares two snapshots, A...B means

93
00:06:31,483 --> 00:06:36,671
"from the merge base of A and B to B".
GitHub's documentation states which one it

94
00:06:36,711 --> 00:06:41,835
uses: "Pull requests on GitHub show a
three-dot diff", and gives the reason: "it

95
00:06:41,875 --> 00:06:44,739
focuses on 'what a pull
request introduces'".

96
00:06:45,079 --> 00:06:49,643
The limit of the three-dot diff, as a
one-line root cause from the textbook: a

97
00:06:49,683 --> 00:06:53,302
three-dot diff is not "what will
change on main when I merge".

98
00:06:53,342 --> 00:06:56,313
It is "what the head changed
since the merge base".

99
00:06:56,353 --> 00:06:59,972
The two differ exactly when both
sides changed the same thing.

100
00:07:00,012 --> 00:07:03,572
The test merge is the object
that answers the first question.

101
00:07:03,912 --> 00:07:07,246
Quick quiz. main gained a
commit after you branched.

102
00:07:07,286 --> 00:07:10,815
Does the "Files changed" tab
show that commit's change?

103
00:07:10,855 --> 00:07:13,216
A: yes, reversed. B: no. Your answer?

104
00:07:18,056 --> 00:07:22,691
B. Three dots start at the merge base,
so later commits on main stay out.

105
00:07:22,731 --> 00:07:25,765
A is what two dots show,
and the demo proves it.

106
00:07:27,605 --> 00:07:32,579
The textbook's analogy: a pull request is
a change request clipped to two bookmarks

107
00:07:32,619 --> 00:07:37,471
in a shared book. The form, who asks, who
must sign, the discussion, lives in the

108
00:07:37,511 --> 00:07:38,861
office's filing system.

109
00:07:39,201 --> 00:07:43,624
The analogy breaks at one point that
matters. The office also keeps a copy of the

110
00:07:43,664 --> 00:07:48,087
proposed pages in its own drawer under
your request number, where you can read it

111
00:07:48,127 --> 00:07:52,054
and cannot change it, and where it
stays after you remove your bookmark.

112
00:07:52,394 --> 00:07:57,038
That drawer is refs/pull/N/head. It is the
reason a deleted branch is not a deleted

113
00:07:57,078 --> 00:07:57,433
commit.

114
00:07:57,773 --> 00:08:02,571
And keep the three commits as a triangle
in your head: base tip, head tip, merge

115
00:08:02,611 --> 00:08:07,288
base. Try it now, thirty seconds, on
paper: draw the two branches and mark the

116
00:08:07,328 --> 00:08:08,316
three. I'll wait.

117
00:08:13,156 --> 00:08:17,944
Here's mine: base tip 9aa221a, head
tip 16d4788, merge base 9a383e5.

118
00:08:17,984 --> 00:08:23,624
Every question about a pull request page
begins with "which of the three moved?"

119
00:08:25,464 --> 00:08:31,278
The top line is main, ending at 9aa221a.
The branch forks at 9a383e5 and has three

120
00:08:31,318 --> 00:08:36,277
commits ending at 16d4788. Two refs
name that commit: your branch, and

121
00:08:36,317 --> 00:08:37,490
refs/pull/1/head.

122
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:43,003
Between them, 135aad1: a merge commit
with two parents, the tip of main and the

123
00:08:43,043 --> 00:08:47,358
head. One ref names it, refs/pull/1/merge,
and it is on no branch.

124
00:08:47,398 --> 00:08:51,779
The bottom line is the GitHub object:
two names and the discussion.

125
00:08:52,119 --> 00:08:55,649
The bracket at the bottom is the
"Commits" tab: three commits.

126
00:08:55,689 --> 00:08:59,046
On the right, the two diffs.
Two dots compare tip with tip.

127
00:08:59,086 --> 00:09:03,596
Three dots compare the merge base with
the head. The "Files changed" tab is the

128
00:09:03,636 --> 00:09:04,402
three-dot one.

129
00:09:06,242 --> 00:09:08,996
On the server, "pull
request 1" becomes a ref.

130
00:09:09,336 --> 00:09:14,882
Two refs name commit 16d4788. receive.hideRefs
is the stock Git setting that hides a

131
00:09:14,922 --> 00:09:18,074
namespace from pushes
while leaving it readable.

132
00:09:20,414 --> 00:09:25,282
The server advertises refs/pull/1/head.
After a plain git fetch, does Asha's clone

133
00:09:25,322 --> 00:09:25,761
have it?

134
00:09:26,101 --> 00:09:31,683
It does not. A clone fetches refs/heads/*
only. To take a pull request into your

135
00:09:31,723 --> 00:09:33,369
clone, you name the ref.

136
00:09:33,709 --> 00:09:39,479
pull/1/head is shorthand for refs/pull/1/head.
gh pr checkout 1 does this fetch for

137
00:09:39,519 --> 00:09:39,759
you.

138
00:09:40,099 --> 00:09:43,515
"Deny updating a hidden ref."
The namespace is read-only.

139
00:09:43,855 --> 00:09:48,733
Now the test merge. A server has no working
tree, so the merge is computed in the

140
00:09:48,773 --> 00:09:53,104
object database, with git merge-tree,
which you met in the merge videos.

141
00:10:03,444 --> 00:10:05,750
Which branches contain
the test merge commit?

142
00:10:06,090 --> 00:10:11,124
None. 135aad1 is a real merge commit
with two parents, reachable only from

143
00:10:11,164 --> 00:10:13,867
refs/pull/1/merge. Neither branch moved.

144
00:10:14,207 --> 00:10:19,065
This is what a CI job does for a pull
request event: it checks out the merge ref.

145
00:10:19,105 --> 00:10:23,902
It tests your commits merged with main as
main was when the test merge was made.

146
00:10:24,242 --> 00:10:29,145
Now one more commit lands on main. Predict:
does the stored test merge follow it?

147
00:10:29,185 --> 00:10:30,121
Say it out loud.

148
00:10:34,961 --> 00:10:40,503
It doesn't. The first parent of the stored
merge is still 9aa221a, and main is at

149
00:10:40,543 --> 00:10:44,294
028b1de. The merge ref is as
fresh as its first parent.

150
00:10:44,634 --> 00:10:49,432
And there's the green check from the hook:
green on a stale test merge, red on the

151
00:10:49,472 --> 00:10:49,963
real one.

152
00:10:50,303 --> 00:10:54,407
A second pull request changes a
line that main has changed since.

153
00:10:54,447 --> 00:10:59,698
git merge-tree exits with status 1. There
is no clean tree, so no test merge commit

154
00:10:59,738 --> 00:11:03,141
and no refs/pull/2/merge.
That is the "when possible".

155
00:11:05,481 --> 00:11:09,949
The head branch is deleted on the server.
Can Ravi still fetch the three commits?

156
00:11:10,289 --> 00:11:15,451
He can: git fetch origin pull/1/head. So
for a leaked secret, deleting the branch

157
00:11:15,491 --> 00:11:18,533
removes nothing. The
credential must be rotated.

158
00:11:35,873 --> 00:11:41,212
The "Commits" tab: three commits. 9aa221a,
the newer commit on main, is not among

159
00:11:41,252 --> 00:11:45,395
them: it is reachable from the
base. The merge base is 9a383e5.

160
00:11:47,735 --> 00:11:52,253
Your branch changed two files. How
many files will the two-dot diff list?

161
00:11:56,793 --> 00:12:01,836
Three. A third file appears, backwards:
the diff claims your branch lowers the

162
00:12:01,876 --> 00:12:05,942
threshold from 0.7 to 0.6. Your
branch never touched that file.

163
00:12:05,982 --> 00:12:11,351
A two-dot diff compares the two tips, so
every change that landed on main after you

164
00:12:11,391 --> 00:12:16,369
branched shows up as its own reversal.
It is what git diff main shows on your

165
00:12:16,409 --> 00:12:21,192
machine, and the usual source of "GitHub
shows something different from my

166
00:12:21,232 --> 00:12:21,844
terminal".

167
00:12:22,184 --> 00:12:26,360
The three-dot diff is a two-dot diff
whose left side is the merge base.

168
00:12:26,700 --> 00:12:31,386
GitHub's advice for keeping the two views
equal is to merge the base into the topic

169
00:12:31,426 --> 00:12:36,168
branch. The merge base moved to the tip of
main, the two diffs agree, and the commit

170
00:12:36,208 --> 00:12:37,705
list gained a merge commit.

171
00:12:38,045 --> 00:12:42,375
Here it is as a picture: the merge base
is now the tip of main, and the list has

172
00:12:42,415 --> 00:12:46,689
four commits. That is the price, and the
next video weighs it against rebasing.

173
00:12:47,029 --> 00:12:50,260
Push a small branch and open
a pull request against main.

174
00:12:50,300 --> 00:12:54,850
On the pull request page, open the tab
that lists commits and the tab that lists

175
00:12:54,890 --> 00:12:58,522
changed files. Before you look,
predict both from your terminal:

176
00:12:58,862 --> 00:13:03,791
Then look for the Git data GitHub created,
and take the pull request into your clone

177
00:13:03,831 --> 00:13:04,620
by its number:

178
00:13:04,960 --> 00:13:10,020
According to the documentation you will
find a head ref for the pull request and,

179
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:15,184
when the merge is clean, a merge ref. Lab
21.1 itself runs the whole cycle locally

180
00:13:15,224 --> 00:13:20,222
in the lab shell with three repositories.
Its step "predict the pull request" is

181
00:13:20,262 --> 00:13:21,103
this exercise.

182
00:13:22,943 --> 00:13:24,591
Five mistakes to watch for.

183
00:13:24,931 --> 00:13:29,434
Believing CI tested the head commit.
Root cause: for a pull request event the

184
00:13:29,474 --> 00:13:34,332
checkout is the test merge, a stored commit
that can be older than the base branch.

185
00:13:34,672 --> 00:13:39,427
Deleting a branch to remove a pushed
secret. Root cause: refs/pull/N/head still

186
00:13:39,467 --> 00:13:43,007
names the commits, and nobody
can delete that ref with Git.

187
00:13:43,347 --> 00:13:47,841
Comparing with git diff main and concluding
that GitHub shows something else.

188
00:13:47,881 --> 00:13:52,788
Root cause: that is a two-dot diff of the
tips; the page shows a three-dot diff from

189
00:13:52,828 --> 00:13:53,671
the merge base.

190
00:13:54,011 --> 00:13:57,714
Reading the "Files changed" tab
as "what will change on main".

191
00:13:57,754 --> 00:14:02,604
Root cause: it is what the head changed
since the merge base; the two differ when

192
00:14:02,644 --> 00:14:04,657
both sides changed the same thing.

193
00:14:04,997 --> 00:14:08,518
Waiting for CI on a pull request
that never starts a run.

194
00:14:08,558 --> 00:14:13,766
Root cause: with a merge conflict there is
no test merge, and pull_request workflows

195
00:14:13,806 --> 00:14:14,453
do not run.

196
00:14:16,293 --> 00:14:21,254
Now, out of the lab. An evaluation job
posts a metric to every pull request of an

197
00:14:21,294 --> 00:14:24,465
LLM application: answer
quality on a fixed test set.

198
00:14:24,505 --> 00:14:28,170
A week later someone tries to
reproduce a number and cannot.

199
00:14:28,210 --> 00:14:33,109
The job recorded the commit it checked
out, and that commit is the test merge: a

200
00:14:33,149 --> 00:14:36,999
commit in nobody's clone, which
may be regenerated at any time.

201
00:14:37,339 --> 00:14:42,265
The fix from the textbook: record
github.event.pull_request.head.sha next to the

202
00:14:42,305 --> 00:14:46,984
metric, or the number cannot be reproduced.
And for the hook, the team stops

203
00:14:47,024 --> 00:14:51,951
treating a green pull request as a statement
about main: the statement was about

204
00:14:51,991 --> 00:14:54,682
main as it was when the
test merge was made.

205
00:14:55,022 --> 00:15:01,072
Before opening any pull request, the team's
habit is three commands: git fetch, git

206
00:15:01,112 --> 00:15:06,355
log --oneline origin/main..HEAD, and
git diff --stat origin/main...HEAD.

207
00:15:06,395 --> 00:15:10,757
A commit you did not write will
be seen by the reviewer too.

208
00:15:12,597 --> 00:15:16,734
Your turn. Do Lab 21.1, "A full
